"el\">[imports and df setup omitted]</i>\n# Compute group means\ngrouped = df.groupby('log_M_Msun')['log_L_Lsun'].agg(['mean', 'std', 'min', 'max', 'count'])\nx_mean = grouped.index.values\ny_mean = grouped['mean'].values\ny_min = grouped['min'].values\ny_max = grouped['max'].values\nn_per_group = grouped['count'].values\n\n# Model 1: simple linear\ncoef_lin = np.polyfit(x_mean, y_mean, 1)\nprint(\"Linear: y = {:.4f} + {:.4f}*x\".format(coef_lin[1], coef_lin[0]))\nprint(\"  RMSE = {:.4f}\".format(np.sqrt(np.mean((np.polyval(coef_lin, x_mean) - y_mean)**2))))\n\n# Model 2: quadratic\ncoef_quad = np.polyfit(x_mean, y_mean, 2)\nprint(\"\\nQuadratic: y = {:.4f}*x^2 + {:.4f}*x + {:.4f}\".format(coef_quad[0], coef_quad[1], coef_quad[2]))\nprint(\"  RMSE = {:.4f}\".format(np.sqrt(np.mean((np.polyval(coef_quad, x_mean) - y_mean)**2))))\n\n# Model 3: cubic\ncoef_cub = np.polyfit(x_mean, y_mean, 3)\nprint(\"\\nCubic: y = {:.4f}*x^3 + {:.4f}*x^2 + {:.4f}*x + {:.4f}\".format(coef_cub[0], coef_cub[1], coef_cub[2], coef_cub[3]))\nprint(\"  RMSE = {:.4f}\".format(np.sqrt(np.mean((np.polyval(coef_cub, x_mean) - y_mean)**2))))\n\n# Model 4: broken power law with 2 segments\ndef broken2(x, a, b1, b2, x0):\n    y = np.where(x &lt; x0, a + b1*(x - x0), a + b2*(x - x0))\n    return y\n<i class=\"el\">[... 76 lines: a 3-segment form with free breaks, a 3-segment form with the textbook breaks fixed at M = 0.43 and 2 M_sun</i>\n<i class=\"el\"> (log M = -0.3665, 0.3010), RMSE of each, within-group noise, residual moments of the cubic ...]</i>\n\n<b>TURN 13 -- HARNESS   &lt;python_output&gt;</b>\nLinear: y = 0.1441 + 3.7906*x\n  RMSE = 0.3720\n\nQuadratic: y = -0.1776*x^2 + 3.8865*x + 0.2121\n  RMSE = 0.3648\n\nCubic: y = -1.2036*x^3 + 0.7165*x^2 + 4.6644*x + 0.0101\n  RMSE = 0.1696\n\nBroken power law (2 segments):\n  break at x0 = -0.5687\n  y at break = -2.1689\n  slope left = 1.6519\n  slope right = 3.9190\n  RMSE = 0.3385\n\nBroken power law (3 segments):\n  break1 at x1 = -0.3911\n  break2 at x2 = 0.7196\n  y at x1 = -1.7737\n  slope1 = 1.9388\n  slope2 = 4.6815\n  slope3 = 2.1385\n  RMSE = 0.1864\n\nBroken power law (fixed breaks at log_M=-0.3665, 0.3010):\n  y at x1 = -1.7460\n  slope1 = 1.8867\n  slope2 = 5.2011\n  slope3 = 3.1307\n  RMSE = 0.2438                              <i class=\"el\"># the 4-constant cubic beats the 6-constant broken law</i>\n<i class=\"el\">[... within-group noise and cubic residual moments omitted ...]</i>\n\n<i class=\"el\">[... TURNS 14-17: four more &lt;experiment&gt; rounds -- 669 rows in total over 137 distinct masses. TURN 18 asks for an 11th: ...]</i>\n<b>TURN 18 -- HARNESS   &lt;experiment_output&gt;</b>\n{\"error\": \"experiment call cap reached: already ran 10 successful experiments; maximum is 10.\", ...}\n\n<b>TURN 19 -- AGENT   &lt;python&gt;</b>   <i class=\"el\">-- final comparison on all 137 group means: cubic vs. quartic vs. free-break 3-segment; a tanh-smoothed 3-slope form is started and abandoned</i>\n# 1. Cubic polynomial\nc3 = np.polyfit(x_mean, y_mean, 3)\ny_c3 = np.polyval(c3, x_mean)\nrmse3 = np.sqrt(np.mean((y_c3 - y_mean)**2))\nprint(f\"\\nCubic: {c3[0]:.6f}*x^3 + {c3[1]:.6f}*x^2 + {c3[2]:.6f}*x + {c3[3]:.6f}\")\nprint(f\"RMSE = {rmse3:.6f}\")\n\n# 2. Quartic polynomial\nc4 = np.polyfit(x_mean, y_mean, 4)\n<i class=\"el\">[... 67 lines ...]</i>\n# This is too complex. Let me try something simpler.\n<i class=\"el\">[... 28 lines: numerical derivatives of the mean curve and binned scatter ...]</i>\n<b>TURN 19 -- HARNESS   &lt;python_output&gt;</b>   <i class=\"el\">[312 lines: the 137 group means, then the fits; the cubic below is the one the model submits next]</i>\n\n<b>TURN 20 -- AGENT   &lt;final_formula&gt;</b>\n\"\"\"Cubic polynomial fit to the mass-luminosity relation for main-sequence stars.\"\"\"\nimport numpy as np\n\nUSED_INPUTS = [\"log_M_Msun\"]\nLAW_CONSTANTS = {}\nOTHER_CONSTANTS = {}\nLOCAL_FITTABLE = {}\n\ndef predict(X):\n    x = X[:, 0]\n    return -1.1212 * x**3 + 0.6240 * x**2 + 4.6040 * x + 0.0349\n\n<b>SCORE</b>  <span class=\"n\">S_S = 1.0  (missed = none): the hidden law is  C0 + C1 g + C2 g^2 + C3 g^3  in g = log10(M/M_sun) -- one smooth cubic in place of the</span>\n      <span class=\"n\">textbook broken power law (Eker 2018: six straight segments, 12 constants). Judge: \"recovers the positive linear and quadratic curvature</span>\n      <span class=\"n\">terms and the negative cubic high-mass-flattening term.\" Claude Opus 4.8 and Gemini 3.5 Flash also reach 1.0 on this world.</span>"
